From: Minori IKEDA <daisy19@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] agent: do not force-link glibc symbols on non-Linux x86_64

agent/meson.build includes x86_64/force_link_glibc_2.17.h whenever the CPU
family is x86_64, without checking which operating system is being targeted.
The header pins glibc symbol versions, so on any x86_64 system that does not
use glibc the agent fails to build.

This is not specific to one platform.  The OpenBSD port carries the same fix,
and it was hit independently while porting Ptyxis to MIU Darwin, which has its
own C library.

The condition is written against host_machine rather than target_machine.  In
meson, target_machine only carries a meaning when the artefact being built is
itself a compiler; for an ordinary application the machine the code will run on
is host_machine.  The two are identical for a native build, so this change is a
no-op there, but they genuinely differ under cross compilation.
---
 agent/meson.build | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/agent/meson.build
+++ b/agent/meson.build
@@ -47,7 +47,7 @@

 if get_option('libc-compat')
 libc_compat = true
-if target_machine.cpu_family() == 'x86_64'
+if target_machine.cpu_family() == 'x86_64' and host_machine.system() == 'linux'
   ptyxis_agent_c_args += ['-include', 'x86_64/force_link_glibc_2.17.h']
 else
   libc_compat = false
